191 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 89 0 R
/T (Objectivism is the concept that it is possible to apply value judgments to art, in other words, some art is' good' and some art is' bad.' Objectivists have a pretty strict definition of what constitutes art, seeing it as something created after a challenging phase. Second, art is normally the domain of a person who has received training. Then this person-the artist-develops a concept applies his or her technical skills and produces an end product. That may be a piece of music, a drawing, a movie, or anything. The product can then be measured by how well-designed it is, its level of originality and the technical skills used to build it.)
/E (Objectivism is the concept that it is possible to apply value judgments to art, in other words, some art is' good' and some art is' bad.' Objectivists have a pretty strict definition of what constitutes art, seeing it as something created after a challenging phase. Second, art is normally the domain of a person who has received training. Then this person-the artist-develops a concept applies his or her technical skills and produces an end product. That may be a piece of music, a drawing, a movie, or anything. The product can then be measured by how well-designed it is, its level of originality and the technical skills used to build it.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
192 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 89 0 R
/T (Subjectivism brings a rather different view of art. Subjectivists argue that there needs to be a broader definition of art, and training is not a necessity. Nearly anything can be regarded as art for strict subjectivists since the most important element in the process of artistic expression is the role of the spectator or audience. In other words, if we experience something when we look at a garbage bag set in a gallery, that's art.)
/E (Subjectivism brings a rather different view of art. Subjectivists argue that there needs to be a broader definition of art, and training is not a necessity. Nearly anything can be regarded as art for strict subjectivists since the most important element in the process of artistic expression is the role of the spectator or audience. In other words, if we experience something when we look at a garbage bag set in a gallery, that's art.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
