197 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 89 0 R
/T (For the entirely different reasons, people seem to dislike art: it just doesn't look very nice; it's not worth a pretty penny; or someone has already told them that is not very good It is also quite easy to compare artworks, and detest one that is based on your thoughts about the piece hung right next to it on the wall.)
/E (For the entirely different reasons, people seem to dislike art: it just doesn't look very nice; it's not worth a pretty penny; or someone has already told them that is not very good It is also quite easy to compare artworks, and detest one that is based on your thoughts about the piece hung right next to it on the wall.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
198 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 89 0 R
/T (Perhaps the easiest reaction to the work can be caused by disinterest or by the viewer deciding that its art idea does not match and diminishes it. It could also come out of a decision against putting in the effort required to understand the artwork, or even just the fact that their interest is not captured by the piece. Interestingly, since art is so ubiquitous, more designers and art critics are beginning to look for a unique art over all else. The art world is almost always looking for the newest, the most audacious, the strangest, even for the obscene and shocking. The artist is indeed a part of the community. And those who are not artists? You don't know why a specific piece of work is unique and sometimes even lose the curiosity and wonder after some time is spent in an art exhibition. We end up looking at price tags rather than creativity and even feel like good art is too complex and abstract to grasp. Nonetheless, what we as patrons of art need to remember is that we are as competent as an art critic to determine if art is good or bad. There is no point in sitting down and gratefully nodding at a piece of trash that hangs in a gallery, if we have already considered it a joke in our head.)
/E (Perhaps the easiest reaction to the work can be caused by disinterest or by the viewer deciding that its art idea does not match and diminishes it. It could also come out of a decision against putting in the effort required to understand the artwork, or even just the fact that their interest is not captured by the piece. Interestingly, since art is so ubiquitous, more designers and art critics are beginning to look for a unique art over all else. The art world is almost always looking for the newest, the most audacious, the strangest, even for the obscene and shocking. The artist is indeed a part of the community. And those who are not artists? You don't know why a specific piece of work is unique and sometimes even lose the curiosity and wonder after some time is spent in an art exhibition. We end up looking at price tags rather than creativity and even feel like good art is too complex and abstract to grasp. Nonetheless, what we as patrons of art need to remember is that we are as competent as an art critic to determine if art is good or bad. There is no point in sitting down and gratefully nodding at a piece of trash that hangs in a gallery, if we have already considered it a joke in our head.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
