1226 0 obj
<<
/Title (Perhaps the easiest reaction to the work can be caused by disinterest or by the viewer deciding that its art idea does not match and diminishes it. It could also come out of a decision against putting in the effort required to understand the artwork, or even just the fact that their interest is not captured by the piece. Interestingly, since art is so ubiquitous, more designers and art critics are beginning to look for a unique art over all else. The art world is almost always looking for the newest, the most audacious, the strangest, even for the obscene and shocking. The artist is indeed a part of the community. And those who are not artists? You don't know why a specific piece of work is unique and sometimes even lose the curiosity and wonder after some time is spent in an art exhibition. We end up looking at price tags rather than creativity and even feel like good art is too complex and abstract to grasp. Nonetheless, what we as patrons of art need to remember is that we are as competent as an art critic to determine if art is good or bad. There is no point in sitting down and gratefully nodding at a piece of trash that hangs in a gallery, if we have already considered it a joke in our head.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1220 0 R
>>
endobj
1227 0 obj
<<
/Title (Objectivism is the concept that it is possible to apply value judgments to art, in other words, some art is' good' and some art is' bad.' Objectivists have a pretty strict definition of what constitutes art, seeing it as something created after a challenging phase. Second, art is normally the domain of a person who has received training. Then this person-the artist-develops a concept applies his or her technical skills and produces an end product. That may be a piece of music, a drawing, a movie, or anything. The product can then be measured by how well-designed it is, its level of originality and the technical skills used to build it.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1221 0 R
/Next 1228 0 R
>>
endobj
1228 0 obj
<<
/Title (Subjectivism brings a rather different view of art. Subjectivists argue that there needs to be a broader definition of art, and training is not a necessity. Nearly anything can be regarded as art for strict subjectivists since the most important element in the process of artistic expression is the role of the spectator or audience. In other words, if we experience something when we look at a garbage bag set in a gallery, that's art.)
/Dest [16 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1221 0 R
/Prev 1227 0 R
>>
endobj
